(inspect client gown/slippers for cleanliness/damage); changed surface disinfectant spec to QAC with contact time; added \"ONE DIRECTION\" wipe to Step 2.2; expanded checklist from 15 to 16 items with new PROCESS item 5 for client items check\n- C02/KK-01: Rewrote Step 1 (1.1–1.6): added PPE donning, QAC + one-direction wipe + air dry, instrument cleaning (BP monitor with 70% IPA), client identity verification, gown provision for physical assessment; expanded checklist from 14 to 17 items with 2 SAFETY items\n- C03/KK-01: Added QAC spec + contact time to Step 2.1; added \"ONE DIRECTION only\" and discard cloth to Step 2.2; added Step 2.3 (clipboard/pen cleaned with 70% IPA); added allergy confirmation as SAFETY item 14 in checklist; expanded from 14 to 15 items\n- C04/KK-01: Added Step 6.6 (pre-treatment metal jewellery removal notice to client); added new Step 7 \"Pre-Client Entry Verification\" (5 sub-steps: identity check, jewellery/implant verbal confirmation, changing, positioning, draping); renamed old Step 7 to Step 8; expanded checklist from 15 to 20 items with 3 new PROCESS items (8–11) and 1 new SAFETY item (18)\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- QAC (quaternary ammonium compound) is the standard surface disinfectant; 70% IPA is the standard for instruments/equipment surfaces — always distinguish these in hygiene steps\n- \"ONE DIRECTION\" wipe is a hygiene principle — critical to include explicitly, not just implied\n- Disinfectant contact time (label time, typically 60 seconds) must be observed — wipe immediately defeats the purpose\n- For electrotherapy KK-01 (work area prep), the pre-treatment client safety check (jewellery, implants, pacemaker) belongs at the END of this KK as the final gate before treatment starts\n\n### Gotchas\n- C04/KK-01 was missing the critical pre-electrotherapy client preparation entirely — metal jewellery on a client during electrical treatment is a serious hazard\n- C02/KK-01 had only 1 SAFETY domain item — always target minimum 